BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:16th Australasian Workshop on Econometrics and Health Economics
DTSTART:20270331T120000
DTEND:20270402T130000
DESCRIPTION:This workshop will provide a forum for the development and dis
 semination of applications of econometrics and other quantitative approach
 es in health economics. It will follow the format of the long-running Euro
 pean workshops on econometrics and health economics.\n\nWe are now calling
  for papers for presentation\, with up to 14 papers to be accepted. Presen
 tations by doctoral students and early career researchers are encouraged.
 \n\nPapers accepted for presentation will each be allocated 50 minutes\, i
 ncluding 30 minutes for the author’s presentation\, 10 minutes for an ap
 pointed discussant and 10 minutes for general discussion.\n\nThe number of
  workshop participants is strictly limited and participants will be expect
 ed to attend the full workshop. <br/>Please see the drop-down below for mo
 re information on papers\, including the submission deadline.\n\nKeynote s
 peakers\n\nProf Alsan is the Annie and Ned Lamont Professor of Internation
 al Studies\, Professor of Economics\, and Thomas J. Davis\, Jr. Faculty Sc
 holar at the Stanford Institute for Economic Policy Research (SIEPR)\, whe
 re she founded and directs the Health Inequality Lab.\n\nPreviously\, she 
 served as the Angelopoulos Professor of Public Policy at Harvard Kennedy S
 chool. Prof Alsan received a BA from Harvard University\, a Master's in pu
 blic health from Harvard School of Public Health\, an MD from Loyola Unive
 rsity\, and a PhD in Economics from Harvard University. She combined the P
 hD with an Infectious Disease Fellowship at Massachusetts General Hospital
 .\n\nProf Alsan trained at Brigham and Women's Hospital\, completing a Hig
 h Global Health Equity Residency Fellowship. Her research on the causes an
 d consequences of health inequality has been recognised with a MacArthur F
 ellowship. She is a member of the National Academy of Medicine and was rec
 ently elected Director for Northern America for the International Health E
 conomics Association.\n\nIn addition to her University of Essex role\, Pro
 f Del Bono is Director of the ESRC Centre for Micro-Social Change (MiSoC).
  As an applied microeconomist\, her research focuses on health\, education
 \, family behavior\, and life-course inequalities.\n\nProf del Bono's work
  employs rigorous econometric methods\, including quasi-experimental desig
 ns\, policy evaluations\, and structural approaches\, leveraging longitudi
 nal surveys\, administrative records\, and primary data to identify causal
  relationships in complex social settings. She investigates how individual
  behavior\, family environments\, and public policies influence health\, h
 uman capital\, and socioeconomic outcomes.\n\nHer recent health economics 
 research focuses on early-life investments\, women's health—specifically
  menopause\, HRT\, and well-being and school-based mental health\, examini
 ng how teacher mental health and school policies impact children.\n\nPubli
 shed in top journals like the <em>Journal of Labor Economics</em> and <em>
 Journal of Health Economics</em>\, Prof del Bono is a Fellow of the UK Aca
 demy of Social Sciences and former President of the European Society for P
 opulation Economics.\n\n<!-- false -->\n<div class="accordion nested-accor
 dion js-frontend-complex-accordion" id="Component-4429809" role="tablist">
 \n<div class="hidden">\n<span class="hidden nested-accordion__expand-text"
 >View</span>\n<span class="hidden nested-accordion__collapse_text">Close</
 span>\n</div>\n<section class="panel section1 Paper_submissions-1 close">
 \n<h3 aria-controls="Paper_submissions-1" aria-expanded="false" class="acc
 Title" data-click-event="monash.accordion.toggle" data-expanded-by-default
 ="false" data-position="1" data-title="Paper submissions" data-type="neste
 d" id="Paper_submissions-1" role="tab">\n<span class="accTitle__icon"><svg
 ><use href="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/git_bridge/0009/2651868/d
 ist/assets/images/icon-spritesheet.svg?h=00fceca7#copy"></use></svg></span
 >\n<span class="accTitle__text">Paper submissions</span>\n<span class="acc
 Title__toggle-wrapper">\n<a class="accTitle__toggle" href="#Paper_submissi
 ons-1" rel="1">\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-text">View</span>\n<span cl
 ass="accTitle__toggle-arrow"><img alt="View" src="https://www.monash.edu/_
 _data/assets/file/0011/2179901/arrow-outline-right.svg"/></span>\n</a>\n</
 span>\n</h3>\n<div aria-labelledby="Paper_submissions-1" class="accContent
 " role="tabpanel" style="display: none\;">\n<div class="accContent__inner"
 >\n<div class="accContent__wrapper">\n<div id="component_4429813">\n<p>Ple
 ase submit a full draft of the paper OR a 1000-word (minimum) abstract by 
 <strong>18 December 2026</strong>.</p><h3>Abstract criteria</h3><p>If send
 ing an abstract\, it should be structured to include <strong>details of th
 e aims</strong>\, the <strong>econometric methods used</strong> (in the pa
 st some abstracts have not contained econometric detail so we have been un
 able to properly assess them)\, and <strong>results</strong>.</p><p>Result
 s should be included even if they are preliminary. Abstracts containing no
  results or no details of the econometric methods will be less likely to b
 e accepted.</p><p>Abstracts and papers will be judged primarily on the qua
 lity of their econometrics content. We strongly encourage presentation and
  discussion of work in progress. Papers already submitted to or accepted b
 y journals are discouraged.</p><h3>Fees for invited participants</h3><div>
 Those invited to participate will be asked to pay the registration fee in 
 February 2027.</div><ul><li>Full registration: AU$650</li><li>PhD students
